       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 4000 mg per dose four times daily is 16,000 mg/day, which is five times the usual maximum for ibuprofen. The maximum is 3,200 mg/day for inpatients and 1,200 mg/day for over-the-counter use. That much ibuprofen would risk serious GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular and CNS toxicity, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nThis looks like a possible decimal or unit error. Did you mean one of these?\n\n- **400 mg PO four times daily** (1,600 mg/day), a common analgesic or anti-inflammatory dose\n- **800 mg PO three times daily** (2,400 mg/day), often used for inflammatory conditions\n- **600 mg PO four times daily** (2,400 mg/day)\n\nIf you really want 4000 mg per dose, tell me the clinical rationale and I'll enter it with a note. Otherwise, tell me which dose you want and I'll enter it right away."
